window.ENDGAME = {"levels":[{"id":"L1","n":1,"name":"The King Wakes Up","tag":"Foundations","c":"var(--guard)","story":"<p>For thirty moves the king hid behind a wall of pawns while the queens and knights did the fighting. Then the queens come off, and something odd happens: the most frightened piece on the board turns into one of the strongest.</p><p>Level 1 is the alphabet of the endgame: how to finish a won game, how a single pawn becomes a queen, and how a lone king can stop it. Every strong player knows these cold, because every other ending eventually turns into one of them.</p>","unlock":0},{"id":"L2","n":2,"name":"Counting and Tempo","tag":"Intermediate","c":"var(--linec)","story":"<p>In the opening, one wasted move rarely matters. In a pawn ending, one move is the whole game. Here you'll count races, sacrifice pawns to make queens, and learn that sometimes the best move is the one that <i>hands the turn to your opponent</i>.</p><p>You'll also meet your first rook ending, the most common ending in chess, and the 250-year-old defence that saves half of them.</p>","unlock":3},{"id":"L3","n":3,"name":"Bridges and Bishops","tag":"Advanced","c":"var(--clock)","story":"<p>Now the pieces come back. A rook builds a bridge for its king. A rook chooses between standing in front of a pawn or behind it, and one of those choices loses. A bishop with an extra pawn can't win because of the colour of one corner square.</p><p>This is where material stops being the score. Position, colour and activity decide.</p>","unlock":3},{"id":"L4","n":4,"name":"The Composers' Magic","tag":"Very advanced","c":"var(--orange)","story":"<p>Endgame composers are the poets of chess. They build positions where the obvious move loses and the impossible one wins: a king that chases two hares at once, squares that explode if you step on them first, a pawn that turns into a rook because a queen would only draw.</p><p>These ideas aren't party tricks. They appear in real games every week, and once you've seen them, you'll never look at a king walk the same way.</p>","unlock":2}],"lessons":[{"id":"qmate","level":"L1","kick":"Lesson 01 · finishing","title":"The shrinking room","metaphor":"Don't chase the cat. Close the doors one at a time.","story":["Try to catch a cat in a big house by running after it and you'll be running all day. The calm way is to close one door, then another, until the cat is in a single small room. Then you walk in.","King and queen against a lone king works the same way. Beginners give check after check and the king just steps aside. A queen that checks is chasing. A queen that <b>takes away squares</b> is closing doors."],"principle":"A lone king can only be mated on the edge of the board. Use the queen to shrink the king's box (a knight's jump away from the king is a handy distance), then bring <b>your own king</b> to help. The queen can never mate alone. And every time the box gets small, check one thing: does he still have a move? If not, and he's not in check, it's stalemate, and your win just became a draw.","tells":["Count the enemy king's legal squares after your move. Fewer is better.","Your king must walk up. The queen alone can't finish.","When the king has only 1–2 squares left, look for stalemate before every move."],"twist":{"q":"Why can't a queen mate a king alone in the middle of the board, even with the king helping only from far away?","a":"A king in the middle has 8 squares. A queen covers a lot, but she can never cover the square next to her that the king could take, and she can't cover all 8 escape squares around a central king. On the edge the king has only 5 squares, in the corner 3, so the queen plus her king can cover them all."},"demos":[{"title":"Close the doors, then call the king","src":"Technique demo · Stockfish-checked shortest win","fen":"8/8/8/4k3/8/8/8/3QK3 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>The black king sits in the middle of the board. It has eight squares and all the space in the world. Watch how many squares it has after each white move.</p>","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"d1d7","s":"Qd7","f":"8/3Q4/8/4k3/8/8/8/4K3 b - - 1 1","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1.Qd7</span> No check. The queen slams two doors at once: the whole 7th rank and the d-file. The black king is now in a box on the right side of the board.</p>","a":[{"u":"d7h7","k":"c"},{"u":"d7d1","k":"c"}]},{"u":"e5f6","s":"Kf6","f":"8/3Q4/5k2/8/8/8/8/4K3 w - - 2 2"},{"u":"e1f2","s":"Kf2","f":"8/3Q4/5k2/8/8/8/5K2/8 b - - 3 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>2.Kf2</span> With the box closed, the queen can wait. The white king walks up the board. This is the part beginners skip.</p>"},{"u":"f6e5","s":"Ke5","f":"8/3Q4/8/4k3/8/8/5K2/8 w - - 4 3"},{"u":"f2f3","s":"Kf3","f":"8/3Q4/8/4k3/8/5K2/8/8 b - - 5 3"},{"u":"e5f6","s":"Kf6","f":"8/3Q4/5k2/8/8/5K2/8/8 w - - 6 4"},{"u":"f3f4","s":"Kf4","f":"8/3Q4/5k2/8/5K2/8/8/8 b - - 7 4","n":"<p><span class='mv'>4.Kf4</span> The kings are getting close. The black king is being squeezed between the queen's wall and the white king.</p>"},{"u":"f6g6","s":"Kg6","f":"8/3Q4/6k1/8/5K2/8/8/8 w - - 8 5"},{"u":"d7e7","s":"Qe7","f":"8/4Q3/6k1/8/5K2/8/8/8 b - - 9 5","n":"<p><span class='mv'>5.Qe7</span> Another door shut. The box is now just the g- and h-files on the 5th and 6th ranks.</p>","a":[{"u":"e7a7","k":"c"}]},{"u":"g6h6","s":"Kh6","f":"8/4Q3/7k/8/5K2/8/8/8 w - - 10 6"},{"u":"f4f5","s":"Kf5","f":"8/4Q3/7k/5K2/8/8/8/8 b - - 11 6","n":"<p><span class='mv'>6.Kf5</span> The white king takes the g-file squares. Black has exactly one move: Kh5. Not stalemate, because there is still a move.</p>"},{"u":"h6h5","s":"Kh5","f":"8/4Q3/8/5K1k/8/8/8/8 w - - 12 7"},{"u":"e7g5","s":"Qg5#","f":"8/8/8/5KQk/8/8/8/8 b - - 13 7","n":"<p><span class='mv'>7.Qg5#</span> The king is on the edge, the queen gives check, and the white king guards the escape squares. Seven moves, and not one wasted check.</p>"}],"mate":true,"end":"8/8/8/5KQk/8/8/8/8 b - - 13 7"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"8/8/2k5/8/8/8/8/5KQ1 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","title":"Corner the king","task":"Checkmate. Stockfish defends.","goal":{"type":"mate","limit":30},"hints":["First shrink the box: a queen move that cuts off a rank or file, without check.","Now walk your king towards his. The queen just waits and keeps the doors shut.","Before every move once the box is tiny: does he still have a legal move?"],"win":"That's the whole technique: doors first, king second, stalemate check always."}},{"id":"rmate","level":"L1","kick":"Lesson 02 · finishing","title":"The rook's box","metaphor":"A rook is a wall. Two kings are a staring contest.","story":["A rook can't cover diagonals, so it can't shrink the box as quickly as a queen. But it's a perfect wall: a straight line the enemy king can never cross.","The trick is that the rook can only push the king back when the two kings stand <b>face to face</b>. Then the rook checks, and the king has to retreat one row. Repeat, row by row, until the edge."],"principle":"Mate with a rook needs teamwork. The rook builds a wall, your king walks up to face the enemy king, and when they stand opposite each other, the rook checks along the wall and pushes him back one row. If he steps sideways instead of facing you, make a quiet <b>waiting move</b> with the rook to keep the wall and hand him the move. It takes up to 16 moves, so be patient.","tells":["The kings are face to face with one square between them: check now.","His king attacks your rook? Move the rook to the far end of its line.","No useful king move? Make a rook waiting move along the wall."],"twist":{"q":"Why does the rook check only when the kings face each other?","a":"A check pushes the king back only if the squares in front of it are taken. When the kings face each other, your king covers the three squares straight ahead, so the check forces a retreat. Any other time, the king just steps forward or sideways and the check was wasted."},"demos":[{"title":"Wall, face-off, check, repeat","src":"Technique demo · Stockfish-checked","fen":"8/8/8/4k3/8/8/8/R3K3 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>Watch the rook build walls and the white king climb. The rook only checks when the kings face each other.</p>","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"a1d1","s":"Rd1","f":"8/8/8/4k3/8/8/8/3RK3 b - - 1 1","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1.Rd1</span> First wall: the d-file. Black's king lives on the right half of the board now.</p>"},{"u":"e5f6","s":"Kf6","f":"8/8/5k2/8/8/8/8/3RK3 w - - 2 2"},{"u":"e1f2","s":"Kf2","f":"8/8/5k2/8/8/8/5K2/3R4 b - - 3 2"},{"u":"f6e5","s":"Ke5","f":"8/8/8/4k3/8/8/5K2/3R4 w - - 4 3"},{"u":"f2e3","s":"Ke3","f":"8/8/8/4k3/8/4K3/8/3R4 b - - 5 3"},{"u":"e5f5","s":"Kf5","f":"8/8/8/5k2/8/4K3/8/3R4 w - - 6 4"},{"u":"d1d4","s":"Rd4","f":"8/8/8/5k2/3R4/4K3/8/8 b - - 7 4","n":"<p><span class='mv'>4.Rd4</span> The wall moves up: the 4th rank is closed too. Black's box shrinks.</p>"},{"u":"f5e5","s":"Ke5","f":"8/8/8/4k3/3R4/4K3/8/8 w - - 8 5"},{"u":"d4d3","s":"Rd3","f":"8/8/8/4k3/8/3RK3/8/8 b - - 9 5","n":"<p><span class='mv'>5.Rd3</span> A <b>waiting move</b>. The rook stays on the d-file and gives Black the move. Every black king move now gives White something.</p>"},{"u":"e5f5","s":"Kf5","f":"8/8/8/5k2/8/3RK3/8/8 w - - 10 6"},{"u":"d3d5","s":"Rd5+","f":"8/8/8/3R1k2/8/4K3/8/8 b - - 11 6","n":"<p><span class='mv'>6.Rd5+</span> Check along the 5th rank, and the king must go back to the 6th. The wall now runs along the 5th rank.</p>"},{"u":"f5e6","s":"Ke6","f":"8/8/4k3/3R4/8/4K3/8/8 w - - 12 7"},{"u":"e3e4","s":"Ke4","f":"8/8/4k3/3R4/4K3/8/8/8 b - - 13 7"},{"u":"e6f6","s":"Kf6","f":"8/8/5k2/3R4/4K3/8/8/8 w - - 14 8"},{"u":"d5e5","s":"Re5","f":"8/8/5k2/4R3/4K3/8/8/8 b - - 15 8","n":"<p><span class='mv'>8.Re5</span> Box: files f–h, ranks 6–8. Notice how the rook keeps its distance from the black king.</p>"},{"u":"f6g6","s":"Kg6","f":"8/8/6k1/4R3/4K3/8/8/8 w - - 16 9"},{"u":"e5f5","s":"Rf5","f":"8/8/6k1/5R2/4K3/8/8/8 b - - 17 9"},{"u":"g6g7","s":"Kg7","f":"8/6k1/8/5R2/4K3/8/8/8 w - - 18 10"},{"u":"e4e5","s":"Ke5","f":"8/6k1/8/4KR2/8/8/8/8 b - - 19 10"},{"u":"g7g6","s":"Kg6","f":"8/8/6k1/4KR2/8/8/8/8 w - - 20 11"},{"u":"e5e6","s":"Ke6","f":"8/8/4K1k1/5R2/8/8/8/8 b - - 21 11"},{"u":"g6g7","s":"Kg7","f":"8/6k1/4K3/5R2/8/8/8/8 w - - 22 12"},{"u":"f5g5","s":"Rg5+","f":"8/6k1/4K3/6R1/8/8/8/8 b - - 23 12","n":"<p><span class='mv'>12.Rg5+</span> The white king guards f7 and f6, so the check along the g-file forces Black to the h-file. The edge.</p>"},{"u":"g7h6","s":"Kh6","f":"8/8/4K2k/6R1/8/8/8/8 w - - 24 13"},{"u":"e6f6","s":"Kf6","f":"8/8/5K1k/6R1/8/8/8/8 b - - 25 13"},{"u":"h6h7","s":"Kh7","f":"8/7k/5K2/6R1/8/8/8/8 w - - 26 14"},{"u":"g5g1","s":"Rg1","f":"8/7k/5K2/8/8/8/8/6R1 b - - 27 14","n":"<p><span class='mv'>14.Rg1</span> Waiting move, far away from the black king. Black must step in front of the white king.</p>"},{"u":"h7h8","s":"Kh8","f":"7k/8/5K2/8/8/8/8/6R1 w - - 28 15"},{"u":"f6f7","s":"Kf7","f":"7k/5K2/8/8/8/8/8/6R1 b - - 29 15"},{"u":"h8h7","s":"Kh7","f":"8/5K1k/8/8/8/8/8/6R1 w - - 30 16"},{"u":"g1h1","s":"Rh1#","f":"8/5K1k/8/8/8/8/8/7R b - - 31 16","n":"<p><span class='mv'>16.Rh1#</span> King on the edge, rook checks along the edge, white king covers g7 and g8. That's the pattern you're aiming for every time.</p>"}],"mate":true,"end":"8/5K1k/8/8/8/8/8/7R b - - 31 16"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"8/8/8/3k4/8/8/8/6KR w - - 0 1","orient":"w","title":"Build the box","task":"Checkmate with king and rook. Stockfish defends.","goal":{"type":"mate","limit":40},"hints":["Cut the king off with the rook along a file or rank first.","Bring your king up until the two kings face each other with one square between them. Then check.","Stuck? A quiet rook move along the same line passes the move to Black."],"win":"Wall, face-off, check. You'll never need more than 16 moves once you trust the rhythm."}},{"id":"square","level":"L1","kick":"Lesson 03 · races","title":"The rule of the square","metaphor":"You don't need to run the race to know who wins it.","story":["A runner leaves the station on foot. You know his speed, your speed and the distance. You don't need to chase him to know whether you'll catch him: one picture of the map tells you.","In chess the picture is a square. Draw a square from the pawn to its promotion rank. If the defending king can step <b>inside</b> that square, it catches the pawn. If not, no amount of running will help. Counting moves in your head is slow and error-prone; the square is instant."],"principle":"A king moves one square at a time in <b>any</b> direction, even diagonally. So a diagonal step gets the king closer to the pawn and closer to its queening square at the same time. Draw the square from the pawn to the last rank (if the pawn is still on its starting rank, count from one square ahead, because it can move two). If it's the defender's move and the king can step into the square, he catches the pawn.","tells":["Count the pawn's distance to promotion; that's the side of the square.","A diagonal king step does two jobs at once.","Whose move it is changes everything. With the pawn's side to move, shrink the square by one first."],"twist":{"q":"A king on h1 and a king on h8: how many moves for each to reach a1 and a8? And how many for the h1 king to reach a8?","a":"7, 7 and 7. The king on h1 can walk the long diagonal to a8 in exactly the same 7 moves it needs to walk straight along the edge. On a chessboard, the diagonal is not longer. That's why diagonal king walks are so sneaky, and Level 4 builds a whole study on it."},"demos":[{"title":"Inside the square","src":"Technique demo · Stockfish-checked","fen":"8/8/5k2/8/P7/8/8/7K b - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>The a-pawn needs four moves to promote. Its square runs from a4 to e8 (the red corners). The black king on f6 is one step outside, but it's Black's move.</p>","a0":[],"m0":["a4","e4","a8","e8"],"steps":[{"u":"f6e6","s":"Ke6","f":"8/8/4k3/8/P7/8/8/7K w - - 1 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1...Ke6</span> One step and the king is inside the square. The race is over before it started.</p>"},{"u":"a4a5","s":"a5","f":"8/8/4k3/P7/8/8/8/7K b - - 0 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>2.a5</span> The square shrinks with the pawn: now a5–d8. The king is still inside.</p>","m":["a5","d5","a8","d8"]},{"u":"e6d6","s":"Kd6","f":"8/8/3k4/P7/8/8/8/7K w - - 1 3"},{"u":"a5a6","s":"a6","f":"8/8/P2k4/8/8/8/8/7K b - - 0 3"},{"u":"d6c7","s":"Kc7","f":"8/2k5/P7/8/8/8/8/7K w - - 1 4","n":"<p><span class='mv'>3...Kc7</span> The king walks diagonally: closer to the pawn and closer to a8 at the same time.</p>"},{"u":"a6a7","s":"a7","f":"8/P1k5/8/8/8/8/8/7K b - - 0 4"},{"u":"c7b7","s":"Kb7","f":"8/Pk6/8/8/8/8/8/7K w - - 1 5"},{"u":"a7a8q","s":"a8=Q+","f":"Q7/1k6/8/8/8/8/8/7K b - - 0 5"},{"u":"b7a8","s":"Kxa8","f":"k7/8/8/8/8/8/8/7K w - - 0 6","n":"<p><span class='mv'>5...Kxa8</span> Caught. If White had been to move at the start, 1.a5 would have shrunk the square to a5–d8 and the king on f6 would have been outside. Same position, different result.</p>"}],"mate":false,"end":"k7/8/8/8/8/8/8/7K w - - 0 6"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"7K/8/8/8/1P6/6k1/8/8 b - - 0 1","orient":"b","title":"Catch the runner","task":"You're Black. Stop the b-pawn. Only one first move works.","goal":{"type":"hold","n":8},"hints":["Draw the square from b4 to the 8th rank: b4–f8.","Which of your king moves lands inside that square?","Diagonal steps are free distance."],"win":"Only 1...Kf4 steps into the square. Every other move is one tempo too slow."}},{"id":"opp","level":"L1","kick":"Lesson 04 · king and pawn","title":"The staring contest","metaphor":"Two people in a narrow corridor. Whoever has to move first, steps aside.","story":["Two people meet in a narrow corridor and stop, face to face. Neither wants to step aside. Whoever is forced to move first has to give way.","When two kings face each other with one square between them, they are in <b>opposition</b>, and the side that does <i>not</i> have to move holds it. Kings can't move next to each other, so the king that must move has to give way, and the other king walks past. In king-and-pawn endings that decides everything."],"principle":"Your king wants to reach the <b>key squares</b> of your pawn: for a pawn that hasn't crossed the middle, the three squares two ranks in front of it (for a pawn on e4: d6, e6 and f6). Get your king there and the pawn promotes no matter what. You get past the defending king by taking the opposition: move so that the kings stand face to face and it's <i>his</i> turn. A pawn move you haven't played yet is a spare tempo, a way to pass the move.","tells":["Kings face each other with one square between: whoever is NOT to move holds the opposition.","Aim for the key squares two ranks ahead of the pawn.","The king goes first; the pawn follows. Pushing too early throws the win away."],"twist":{"q":"Why is a pawn on its starting square sometimes worth more than an advanced one in these endings?","a":"Because it can move one square or two. That gives you a choice of tempo: a spare move to pass the turn to your opponent at exactly the right moment and win the opposition back."},"demos":[{"title":"Same position, Black to move: White wins","src":"Technique demo · Stockfish-checked","fen":"8/4k3/8/4K3/4P3/8/8/8 b - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>The kings face each other on e5 and e7. It's <b>Black's</b> turn, so White holds the opposition. The key squares of the e4-pawn are d6, e6 and f6 (marked). Black must step aside, and the white king will walk onto one of them.</p>","a0":[],"m0":["d6","e6","f6"],"steps":[{"u":"e7f7","s":"Kf7","f":"8/5k2/8/4K3/4P3/8/8/8 w - - 1 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1...Kf7</span> Black had to move and gave way to one side.</p>"},{"u":"e5d6","s":"Kd6","f":"8/5k2/3K4/8/4P3/8/8/8 b - - 2 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>2.Kd6</span> The white king walks onto a key square. From here the pawn can't be stopped.</p>"},{"u":"f7f8","s":"Kf8","f":"5k2/8/3K4/8/4P3/8/8/8 w - - 3 3"},{"u":"d6d7","s":"Kd7","f":"5k2/3K4/8/8/4P3/8/8/8 b - - 4 3","n":"<p><span class='mv'>3.Kd7</span> The king clears the road. Notice the pawn still hasn't moved: the king goes first, the pawn follows.</p>"},{"u":"f8g8","s":"Kg8","f":"6k1/3K4/8/8/4P3/8/8/8 w - - 5 4"},{"u":"e4e5","s":"e5","f":"6k1/3K4/8/4P3/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 4"},{"u":"g8f7","s":"Kf7","f":"8/3K1k2/8/4P3/8/8/8/8 w - - 1 5"},{"u":"e5e6","s":"e6+","f":"8/3K1k2/4P3/8/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 5"},{"u":"f7f6","s":"Kf6","f":"8/3K4/4Pk2/8/8/8/8/8 w - - 1 6"},{"u":"e6e7","s":"e7","f":"8/3KP3/5k2/8/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 6"},{"u":"f6e5","s":"Ke5","f":"8/3KP3/8/4k3/8/8/8/8 w - - 1 7"},{"u":"e7e8q","s":"e8=Q+","f":"4Q3/3K4/8/4k3/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 7","n":"<p><span class='mv'>7.e8=Q+</span> Promoted. Now look at Lesson 5: the exact same position with White to move is a draw.</p>"}],"mate":false,"end":"4Q3/3K4/8/4k3/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 7"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"8/8/8/4k3/8/3K4/4P3/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","title":"Win the staring contest","task":"Win: promote the pawn and keep your queen. Only one first move works.","goal":{"type":"promote"},"hints":["Where do you want your king to stand so that the kings face each other and it's Black's turn?","Your pawn on e2 is a spare tempo. Don't spend it yet.","Try 1.Ke3. Then whenever Black steps aside, walk past him."],"win":"1.Ke3! takes the opposition straight away, and the untouched e-pawn keeps a spare tempo in reserve for later."}},{"id":"wall","level":"L1","kick":"Lesson 05 · king and pawn","title":"Standing in the doorway","metaphor":"You can't win the fight, but you can block the door.","story":["A bigger opponent wants to get through a door. You can't beat him, but you can stand in the doorway, and if you move at the right moments, he never gets past.","The defending king against king and pawn is that person in the doorway. It stands in front of the pawn and uses the opposition the other way round. If the attacker gets everything right, the defence ends in a famous trap: stalemate."],"principle":"Defending king and pawn against king: stand <b>directly in front of the pawn</b>, and when the attacking king comes forward, step in front of it to take the opposition. When the pawn reaches your back rank area, retreat <i>straight back</i>, not to the side. In the end the attacker is left with a stalemate or a pawn that can't advance. Rook pawns (a- and h-pawns) are almost always a draw if the defender reaches the corner.","tells":["Stand in front of the pawn, not beside it.","Answer his king move with the opposition.","Retreat straight back towards the promotion square."],"twist":{"q":"In the demo, why did Black retreat to e8 and not to d8 or f8?","a":"Straight back keeps Black in front of the pawn. From e8, Black can answer Kd6 with Kd8 and Kf6 with Kf8, taking the opposition again. A sideways retreat lets the white king step in front of the pawn onto the 7th rank, and then the pawn promotes."},"demos":[{"title":"White to move: Black holds","src":"Technique demo · Stockfish-checked","fen":"8/4k3/8/4K3/4P3/8/8/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>The same position as Lesson 4, but now it's <b>White's</b> turn, so Black holds the opposition. Watch Black keep stepping in front.</p>","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"e5d5","s":"Kd5","f":"8/4k3/8/3K4/4P3/8/8/8 b - - 1 1","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1.Kd5</span> White tries to go round.</p>"},{"u":"e7d7","s":"Kd7","f":"8/3k4/8/3K4/4P3/8/8/8 w - - 2 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1...Kd7</span> Black steps in front: opposition again.</p>"},{"u":"e4e5","s":"e5","f":"8/3k4/8/3KP3/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 2"},{"u":"d7e7","s":"Ke7","f":"8/4k3/8/3KP3/8/8/8/8 w - - 1 3"},{"u":"e5e6","s":"e6","f":"8/4k3/4P3/3K4/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 3"},{"u":"e7e8","s":"Ke8","f":"4k3/8/4P3/3K4/8/8/8/8 w - - 1 4","n":"<p><span class='mv'>3...Ke8!</span> The key moment: straight back. 3...Kd8? 4.Kd6 or 3...Kf8? 4.Kd7 would lose.</p>"},{"u":"d5d6","s":"Kd6","f":"4k3/8/3KP3/8/8/8/8/8 b - - 2 4"},{"u":"e8d8","s":"Kd8","f":"3k4/8/3KP3/8/8/8/8/8 w - - 3 5","n":"<p><span class='mv'>4...Kd8</span> Opposition once more. White can't make progress without pushing.</p>"},{"u":"e6e7","s":"e7+","f":"3k4/4P3/3K4/8/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 5"},{"u":"d8e8","s":"Ke8","f":"4k3/4P3/3K4/8/8/8/8/8 w - - 1 6"},{"u":"d6e6","s":"Ke6","f":"4k3/4P3/4K3/8/8/8/8/8 b - - 2 6","n":"<p><span class='mv'>6.Ke6</span> Black has no legal move and isn't in check: <b>stalemate</b>. White had no better try, because any other king move lets Black take the pawn on e7. Draw.</p>"}],"mate":false,"end":"4k3/4P3/4K3/8/8/8/8/8 b - - 2 6"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"8/8/4k3/8/4PK2/8/8/8 b - - 0 1","orient":"b","title":"Hold the door","task":"You're Black. Draw. Only one move holds right now.","goal":{"type":"hold","n":12},"hints":["White's king wants to reach d6, e6 or f6 later. Stand in its way.","Which move puts your king face to face with White's king, with White to move?","1...Kf6! is the opposition."],"win":"1...Kf6 takes the opposition. From there, keep facing the white king and retreat straight back."}},{"id":"outside","level":"L2","kick":"Lesson 06 · pawn endings","title":"The decoy","metaphor":"Send one soldier far away. The whole enemy army follows him.","story":["A magician waves his left hand high in the air so the audience watches it, while the right hand does the trick.","An <b>outside passed pawn</b> is the waving hand. It's a passed pawn far from the other pawns. The enemy king has to run over and stop it, and while he's away, your king walks into his pawns on the other wing and eats them."],"principle":"In pawn endings, the king that is <b>closer to the action</b> wins. A passed pawn far away from everything else drags the enemy king away from the centre. You may lose the pawn, but your king gains several tempi, and those tempi are spent eating the pawns he left behind. Equal material can still be a completely winning position.","tells":["A passed pawn on the wing farthest from the other pawns.","Count: how many moves for his king to take it, and for yours to reach his pawns?","Your king goes to the side he abandoned, not after the pawn."],"twist":{"q":"Why is a passed pawn on the a-file usually stronger than a passed pawn on the d-file in a pawn ending?","a":"Distance. To stop an a-pawn, the enemy king has to go all the way to the edge, far from the centre and from his other pawns. A d-pawn can be stopped by a king standing in the middle, where it also protects everything else."},"demos":[{"title":"Wave the a-pawn, eat the kingside","src":"Technique demo · Stockfish-checked","fen":"8/6pp/5p2/3k4/P7/3K2P1/7P/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>Three pawns each. But White's a-pawn has no black pawn anywhere near it. It's an outside passed pawn.</p>","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"a4a5","s":"a5","f":"8/6pp/5p2/P2k4/8/3K2P1/7P/8 b - - 0 1","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1.a5</span> The decoy runs. Black can't ignore it: it promotes in three moves.</p>","a":[{"u":"a5a8","k":"b"}]},{"u":"d5c6","s":"Kc6","f":"8/6pp/2k2p2/P7/8/3K2P1/7P/8 w - - 1 2"},{"u":"d3e4","s":"Ke4","f":"8/6pp/2k2p2/P7/4K3/6P1/7P/8 b - - 2 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>2.Ke4</span> White's king does <b>not</b> follow the pawn. It heads to the other side.</p>","a":[{"u":"e4f5","k":"c"}]},{"u":"c6b5","s":"Kb5","f":"8/6pp/5p2/Pk6/4K3/6P1/7P/8 w - - 3 3"},{"u":"e4f5","s":"Kf5","f":"8/6pp/5p2/Pk3K2/8/6P1/7P/8 b - - 4 3"},{"u":"b5a5","s":"Kxa5","f":"8/6pp/5p2/k4K2/8/6P1/7P/8 w - - 0 4","n":"<p><span class='mv'>3...Kxa5</span> Black has won the pawn. But look where his king is: on a5, as far from the kingside as possible.</p>"},{"u":"f5e6","s":"Ke6","f":"8/6pp/4Kp2/k7/8/6P1/7P/8 b - - 1 4"},{"u":"f6f5","s":"f5","f":"8/6pp/4K3/k4p2/8/6P1/7P/8 w - - 0 5"},{"u":"e6f5","s":"Kxf5","f":"8/6pp/8/k4K2/8/6P1/7P/8 b - - 0 5","n":"<p><span class='mv'>5.Kxf5</span> The feast begins.</p>"},{"u":"a5b6","s":"Kb6","f":"8/6pp/1k6/5K2/8/6P1/7P/8 w - - 1 6"},{"u":"f5e6","s":"Ke6","f":"8/6pp/1k2K3/8/8/6P1/7P/8 b - - 2 6"},{"u":"b6b5","s":"Kb5","f":"8/6pp/4K3/1k6/8/6P1/7P/8 w - - 3 7"},{"u":"e6f7","s":"Kf7","f":"8/5Kpp/8/1k6/8/6P1/7P/8 b - - 4 7"},{"u":"h7h5","s":"h5","f":"8/5Kp1/8/1k5p/8/6P1/7P/8 w - - 0 8"},{"u":"f7g7","s":"Kxg7","f":"8/6K1/8/1k5p/8/6P1/7P/8 b - - 0 8","n":"<p><span class='mv'>8.Kxg7</span> Another one.</p>"},{"u":"b5a4","s":"Ka4","f":"8/6K1/8/7p/k7/6P1/7P/8 w - - 1 9"},{"u":"g7g6","s":"Kg6","f":"8/8/6K1/7p/k7/6P1/7P/8 b - - 2 9"},{"u":"a4b3","s":"Kb3","f":"8/8/6K1/7p/8/1k4P1/7P/8 w - - 3 10"},{"u":"g6h5","s":"Kxh5","f":"8/8/8/7K/8/1k4P1/7P/8 b - - 0 10","n":"<p><span class='mv'>10.Kxh5</span> All three black pawns are gone. White's g- and h-pawns will promote. The pawn White gave up bought five free moves.</p>"}],"mate":false,"end":"8/8/8/7K/8/1k4P1/7P/8 b - - 0 10"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"8/pp6/2p5/4k3/7P/1P2K3/P7/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","title":"Mirror trick","task":"Win: promote a pawn and keep the queen. Use your outside passed pawn.","goal":{"type":"promote"},"hints":["Your h-pawn is the outside passed pawn. It will drag his king to the kingside.","Don't run after it with your own king. Where are his pawns?","Push the h-pawn, then march your king to the queenside."],"win":"Decoy on one wing, feast on the other. That's how equal material becomes a win."}},{"id":"detour","level":"L2","kick":"Lesson 07 · pawn endings","title":"The long way round","metaphor":"When the front door is locked, walk around the house.","story":["A burglar finds the front door locked. He doesn't kick it. He walks around to the back window.","When kings face each other and neither can pass, the attacker has another option: <b>outflanking</b>. His king walks around the side, and the defender, who has to keep guarding his pawn, can't follow without abandoning something. Sometimes the shortest road to a square is not the straight one."],"principle":"A defending king that guards a pawn is tied to the squares around it. Walk your king around to the other side of that pawn, and the defender runs out of safe squares. A king that has to move but has no good square is in <b>zugzwang</b>, German for \"compulsion to move\". In endgames, zugzwang is one of your main weapons.","tells":["His king is tied to defending one pawn.","Straight approach blocked? Look for a route around the side.","Count his safe squares. When you take the last one, it's zugzwang."],"twist":{"q":"In the demo, what would happen if White had played 3.Kd6 when it was White's move again in the same position?","a":"Nothing, which is exactly the point of zugzwang. The position itself isn't winning; having the opponent to move in it is. Level 4 takes this idea to its extreme: squares where whoever steps first loses."},"demos":[{"title":"Around the back","src":"Technique demo · Stockfish-checked","fen":"8/2k5/2p5/2PK4/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>White's king attacks c6, Black's king defends it. A head-on approach goes nowhere. So the white king goes around.</p>","a0":[{"u":"d5e6","k":"c"},{"u":"e6d7","k":"c"}],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"d5e6","s":"Ke6","f":"8/2k5/2p1K3/2P5/8/8/8/8 b - - 1 1","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1.Ke6</span> Away from the pawn, but heading for d7: the back door.</p>"},{"u":"c7b8","s":"Kb8","f":"1k6/8/2p1K3/2P5/8/8/8/8 w - - 2 2"},{"u":"e6d7","s":"Kd7","f":"1k6/3K4/2p5/2P5/8/8/8/8 b - - 3 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>2.Kd7</span> The white king is now behind the black king's line. Black's king has to stay on b7 or b8 near c6.</p>"},{"u":"b8b7","s":"Kb7","f":"8/1k1K4/2p5/2P5/8/8/8/8 w - - 4 3"},{"u":"d7d6","s":"Kd6","f":"8/1k6/2pK4/2P5/8/8/8/8 b - - 5 3","n":"<p><span class='mv'>3.Kd6</span> Zugzwang. If it were White's turn, nothing would happen. But Black must move, and every king move abandons c6.</p>"},{"u":"b7b8","s":"Kb8","f":"1k6/8/2pK4/2P5/8/8/8/8 w - - 6 4"},{"u":"d6c6","s":"Kxc6","f":"1k6/8/2K5/2P5/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 4","n":"<p><span class='mv'>4.Kxc6</span> The pawn falls, and the white king stands in front of its own pawn. The rest is Lesson 4.</p>"},{"u":"b8a7","s":"Ka7","f":"8/k7/2K5/2P5/8/8/8/8 w - - 1 5"},{"u":"c6d6","s":"Kd6","f":"8/k7/3K4/2P5/8/8/8/8 b - - 2 5"},{"u":"a7a6","s":"Ka6","f":"8/8/k2K4/2P5/8/8/8/8 w - - 3 6"},{"u":"c5c6","s":"c6","f":"8/8/k1PK4/8/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 6"},{"u":"a6b5","s":"Kb5","f":"8/8/2PK4/1k6/8/8/8/8 w - - 1 7"},{"u":"c6c7","s":"c7","f":"8/2P5/3K4/1k6/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 7","n":"<p><span class='mv'>7.c7</span> The pawn promotes next move.</p>"}],"mate":false,"end":"8/2P5/3K4/1k6/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 7"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"8/1k6/2p5/2P5/3K4/8/8/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","title":"Find the back door","task":"Win: promote and keep the queen. Only one first move wins.","goal":{"type":"promote"},"hints":["Heading straight for c6 via c4 or b4 doesn't work. Black just mirrors you.","Look at the other side of the pawn: d7 and d6.","1.Ke5! starts the walk around."],"win":"1.Ke5! The long way round is the only way."}},{"id":"break","level":"L2","kick":"Lesson 08 · pawn endings","title":"The breakthrough","metaphor":"Three soldiers sacrifice two so that one walks through.","story":["Three soldiers face three soldiers across a trench. Nobody can advance. Then the middle soldier charges and dies, the second charges and dies, and the third walks through the gap they opened.","That's the pawn breakthrough. Three pawns against three, face to face on the 5th and 7th ranks: a sacrifice in the middle and another on the side, and one pawn runs through to promote before the enemy king can arrive."],"principle":"Pawns that face each other look locked, but a pawn sacrifice can force the opponent's pawns off their files. If the enemy king is far away, the pawn that breaks through promotes before anyone can stop it. Always count: is his king far enough? And never allow this pattern against <i>yourself</i>: if you see it coming, push one of your pawns first to break the formation.","tells":["Your pawns are further advanced than his and face them directly.","His king is too far to catch the pawn that breaks through.","The sacrifice starts in the middle."],"twist":{"q":"In the starting demo position, what should Black play if it were Black's move?","a":"1...b6! breaks up the formation before White can sacrifice. After the pawn exchanges, nothing breaks through. The best defence against a breakthrough is to see it a move earlier."},"demos":[{"title":"1.b6!!","src":"Classic pattern · Stockfish-checked","fen":"6k1/ppp5/8/PPP5/8/8/8/6K1 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>Three against three on the queenside, kings far away on the kingside. Ordinary moves draw. One move wins.</p>","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"b5b6","s":"b6","f":"6k1/ppp5/1P6/P1P5/8/8/8/6K1 b - - 0 1","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1.b6!!</span> The middle pawn charges. If 1...cxb6, then 2.a6! bxa6 3.c6 and the c-pawn runs through instead. Mirror image.</p>"},{"u":"a7b6","s":"axb6","f":"6k1/1pp5/1p6/P1P5/8/8/8/6K1 w - - 0 2"},{"u":"c5c6","s":"c6","f":"6k1/1pp5/1pP5/P7/8/8/8/6K1 b - - 0 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>2.c6!</span> The second sacrifice.</p>"},{"u":"b7c6","s":"bxc6","f":"6k1/2p5/1pp5/P7/8/8/8/6K1 w - - 0 3"},{"u":"a5a6","s":"a6","f":"6k1/2p5/Ppp5/8/8/8/8/6K1 b - - 0 3","n":"<p><span class='mv'>3.a6</span> Nothing can stop the a-pawn. The black b-pawns are on the wrong files.</p>"},{"u":"g8f7","s":"Kf7","f":"8/2p2k2/Ppp5/8/8/8/8/6K1 w - - 1 4"},{"u":"a6a7","s":"a7","f":"8/P1p2k2/1pp5/8/8/8/8/6K1 b - - 0 4"},{"u":"f7e7","s":"Ke7","f":"8/P1p1k3/1pp5/8/8/8/8/6K1 w - - 1 5"},{"u":"a7a8q","s":"a8=Q","f":"Q7/2p1k3/1pp5/8/8/8/8/6K1 b - - 0 5","n":"<p><span class='mv'>5.a8=Q</span> A new queen, while the black king is still two moves away.</p>"}],"mate":false,"end":"Q7/2p1k3/1pp5/8/8/8/8/6K1 b - - 0 5"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"1k6/5ppp/8/5PPP/8/8/8/1K6 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","title":"Break through on the kingside","task":"Win: promote and keep the queen. The same pattern, on the other wing.","goal":{"type":"promote"},"hints":["The kings are far away on the queenside.","Start the sacrifice in the middle.","1.g6! and answer each capture with the next sacrifice."],"win":"1.g6! The pattern is the same on any wing."}},{"id":"qvp","level":"L2","kick":"Lesson 09 · queen against pawn","title":"The staircase","metaphor":"Every check is a step. Every block buys your king one step closer.","story":["A pawn on the 7th rank, protected by its king, one move from becoming a queen. You have a queen already, but your king is miles away.","The queen can't take the pawn yet, and she can't stop it alone forever. So she climbs down a staircase of checks, until the enemy king has to step <b>in front</b> of his own pawn. That blocks the pawn for one move, and your king uses that move to come one step closer. Repeat."],"principle":"Against a centre or knight pawn on the 7th, the queen gives checks and pins until the defending king is forced onto the promotion square in front of the pawn. That gains one tempo for the attacking king. Repeat until the king arrives and the pawn falls. Against a <b>rook pawn or bishop pawn</b>, the defender has a stalemate trick: he walks into the corner and, if you take the time to bring your king, he's stalemated. Those are usually draws.","tells":["Check from a square that forces his king onto the queening square.","When his king blocks his own pawn, you get one free king move.","Bishop pawn or rook pawn? Look for stalemate tricks before you celebrate."],"twist":{"q":"Why does the same method fail against a pawn on c2 with the king on b1 able to go to a1?","a":"When you check and the king goes to a1 instead of blocking, taking the pawn isn't possible and you have no time for a king move: if you don't keep checking, the pawn queens. And if you stop to bring your king, it's stalemate, because the black king in the corner has no moves while the pawn is blocked. That's why bishop and rook pawns usually draw."},"demos":[{"title":"Down the staircase","src":"Technique demo · Stockfish-checked","fen":"K7/6Q1/8/8/8/8/3pk3/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>Black threatens d1=Q. The white king on a8 is far away. Watch the rhythm: checks until the black king blocks d1, then one white king move.</p>","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"g7g4","s":"Qg4+","f":"K7/8/8/8/6Q1/8/3pk3/8 b - - 1 1"},{"u":"e2e1","s":"Ke1","f":"K7/8/8/8/6Q1/8/3p4/4k3 w - - 2 2"},{"u":"g4e4","s":"Qe4+","f":"K7/8/8/8/4Q3/8/3p4/4k3 b - - 3 2"},{"u":"e1f1","s":"Kf1","f":"K7/8/8/8/4Q3/8/3p4/5k2 w - - 4 3"},{"u":"e4f3","s":"Qf3+","f":"K7/8/8/8/8/5Q2/3p4/5k2 b - - 5 3"},{"u":"f1e1","s":"Ke1","f":"K7/8/8/8/8/5Q2/3p4/4k3 w - - 6 4"},{"u":"f3e3","s":"Qe3+","f":"K7/8/8/8/8/4Q3/3p4/4k3 b - - 7 4","n":"<p><span class='mv'>4.Qe3+</span> The check forces the king onto d1, in front of its own pawn.</p>"},{"u":"e1d1","s":"Kd1","f":"K7/8/8/8/8/4Q3/3p4/3k4 w - - 8 5","n":"<p><span class='mv'>4...Kd1</span> The pawn is blocked for one move.</p>"},{"u":"a8b7","s":"Kb7","f":"8/1K6/8/8/8/4Q3/3p4/3k4 b - - 9 5","n":"<p><span class='mv'>5.Kb7</span> The free move. The white king takes its first step.</p>"},{"u":"d1c2","s":"Kc2","f":"8/1K6/8/8/8/4Q3/2kp4/8 w - - 10 6"},{"u":"e3e4","s":"Qe4+","f":"8/1K6/8/8/4Q3/8/2kp4/8 b - - 11 6"},{"u":"c2c1","s":"Kc1","f":"8/1K6/8/8/4Q3/8/3p4/2k5 w - - 12 7"},{"u":"e4c4","s":"Qc4+","f":"8/1K6/8/8/2Q5/8/3p4/2k5 b - - 13 7"},{"u":"c1b1","s":"Kb1","f":"8/1K6/8/8/2Q5/8/3p4/1k6 w - - 14 8"},{"u":"c4d3","s":"Qd3+","f":"8/1K6/8/8/8/3Q4/3p4/1k6 b - - 15 8"},{"u":"b1c1","s":"Kc1","f":"8/1K6/8/8/8/3Q4/3p4/2k5 w - - 16 9"},{"u":"d3c3","s":"Qc3+","f":"8/1K6/8/8/8/2Q5/3p4/2k5 b - - 17 9"},{"u":"c1d1","s":"Kd1","f":"8/1K6/8/8/8/2Q5/3p4/3k4 w - - 18 10","n":"<p><span class='mv'>9...Kd1</span> Same trick again: checks until the king blocks d1.</p>"},{"u":"b7b6","s":"Kb6","f":"8/8/1K6/8/8/2Q5/3p4/3k4 b - - 19 10","n":"<p><span class='mv'>10.Kb6</span> Second free step.</p>"},{"u":"d1e2","s":"Ke2","f":"8/8/1K6/8/8/2Q5/3pk3/8 w - - 20 11"},{"u":"c3c2","s":"Qc2","f":"8/8/1K6/8/8/8/2Qpk3/8 b - - 21 11"},{"u":"e2e1","s":"Ke1","f":"8/8/1K6/8/8/8/2Qp4/4k3 w - - 22 12"},{"u":"c2e4","s":"Qe4+","f":"8/8/1K6/8/4Q3/8/3p4/4k3 b - - 23 12"},{"u":"e1f2","s":"Kf2","f":"8/8/1K6/8/4Q3/8/3p1k2/8 w - - 24 13"},{"u":"e4d3","s":"Qd3","f":"8/8/1K6/8/8/3Q4/3p1k2/8 b - - 25 13"},{"u":"f2e1","s":"Ke1","f":"8/8/1K6/8/8/3Q4/3p4/4k3 w - - 26 14"},{"u":"d3e3","s":"Qe3+","f":"8/8/1K6/8/8/4Q3/3p4/4k3 b - - 27 14"},{"u":"e1d1","s":"Kd1","f":"8/8/1K6/8/8/4Q3/3p4/3k4 w - - 28 15"},{"u":"b6c5","s":"Kc5","f":"8/8/8/2K5/8/4Q3/3p4/3k4 b - - 29 15","n":"<p><span class='mv'>15.Kc5</span> Third step. The king is getting close.</p>"},{"u":"d1c2","s":"Kc2","f":"8/8/8/2K5/8/4Q3/2kp4/8 w - - 30 16"},{"u":"e3e2","s":"Qe2","f":"8/8/8/2K5/8/8/2kpQ3/8 b - - 31 16"},{"u":"c2c1","s":"Kc1","f":"8/8/8/2K5/8/8/3pQ3/2k5 w - - 32 17"},{"u":"e2c4","s":"Qc4+","f":"8/8/8/2K5/2Q5/8/3p4/2k5 b - - 33 17"},{"u":"c1b1","s":"Kb1","f":"8/8/8/2K5/2Q5/8/3p4/1k6 w - - 34 18"},{"u":"c4d3","s":"Qd3+","f":"8/8/8/2K5/8/3Q4/3p4/1k6 b - - 35 18"},{"u":"b1c1","s":"Kc1","f":"8/8/8/2K5/8/3Q4/3p4/2k5 w - - 36 19"},{"u":"d3c3","s":"Qc3+","f":"8/8/8/2K5/8/2Q5/3p4/2k5 b - - 37 19"},{"u":"c1d1","s":"Kd1","f":"8/8/8/2K5/8/2Q5/3p4/3k4 w - - 38 20"},{"u":"c5d4","s":"Kd4","f":"8/8/8/8/3K4/2Q5/3p4/3k4 b - - 39 20","n":"<p><span class='mv'>20.Kd4</span> The king arrives.</p>"},{"u":"d1e2","s":"Ke2","f":"8/8/8/8/3K4/2Q5/3pk3/8 w - - 40 21"},{"u":"c3e3","s":"Qe3+","f":"8/8/8/8/3K4/4Q3/3pk3/8 b - - 41 21"},{"u":"e2f1","s":"Kf1","f":"8/8/8/8/3K4/4Q3/3p4/5k2 w - - 42 22"},{"u":"e3d2","s":"Qxd2","f":"8/8/8/8/3K4/8/3Q4/5k2 b - - 0 22","n":"<p><span class='mv'>22.Qxd2</span> The pawn falls. Now it's simply king and queen against king: Lesson 1.</p>"}],"mate":false,"end":"8/8/8/8/3K4/8/3Q4/5k2 b - - 0 22"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"7K/8/8/8/8/7Q/1pk5/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","title":"Win the knight pawn","task":"Win the b-pawn (keep your queen). Stockfish defends.","goal":{"type":"material"},"hints":["Check the king until it has to stand on b1, in front of its pawn.","When the pawn is blocked, use that one move to bring your king closer.","If the king ever leaves the pawn unprotected, take it."],"win":"Staircase, block, king step. A knight pawn on the 7th can't escape that rhythm."}},{"id":"philidor","level":"L2","kick":"Lesson 10 · rook endings","title":"The third-rank fence","metaphor":"Keep the enemy out of your garden. If he comes in, shoot from behind.","story":["Most endgames you'll ever play are rook endings. And the most common one is a rook and pawn against a rook. Before you learn to win it, learn to save it.","François-André Philidor, the best player of the 18th century, showed the defence. Your rook builds a fence on your 3rd rank (the 6th for Black), and the attacking king can't come forward. If the pawn advances to make room, the fence has done its job: your rook drops to the back and checks from behind, and there's nowhere to hide."],"principle":"With the defending king in front of the pawn, put your rook on your <b>third rank</b> and keep it there. The attacking king can't cross it without being checked. The attacker's only try is to push the pawn to that rank, but then his king loses its shelter in front of the pawn. That's your signal: rook to the far back rank, and check, check, check from behind.","tells":["Your king stands in front of the pawn.","Rook on your 3rd rank. Wait there.","The pawn steps onto the 3rd rank? Rook to the back, check from behind."],"twist":{"q":"Why does the defence fail if the defending rook stays passive on the back rank from the start?","a":"Then the attacking king can come to the 6th rank in front of the pawn, sheltered from checks by its own pawn, and the defending king gets pushed off the queening square. The fence on the 3rd rank stops the king <i>before</i> it reaches that shelter."},"demos":[{"title":"Fence, then checks from behind","src":"Philidor's defence · Stockfish-checked","fen":"4k3/R7/1r6/3KP3/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 1","orient":"b","n0":"<p>Black's king stands in front of the e-pawn. The black rook on the 6th rank is the fence.</p>","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"b6h6","s":"Rh6","f":"4k3/R7/7r/3KP3/8/8/8/8 w - - 1 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1...Rh6</span> Just wait on the 6th. The white king can't go to d6 or e6.</p>"},{"u":"e5e6","s":"e6","f":"4k3/R7/4P2r/3K4/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>2.e6</span> White pushes to make progress. But now the pawn stands on e6, and the white king has no shelter from checks.</p>"},{"u":"h6h1","s":"Rh1","f":"4k3/R7/4P3/3K4/8/8/8/7r w - - 1 3","n":"<p><span class='mv'>2...Rh1!</span> Now the rook goes to the back.</p>"},{"u":"d5d6","s":"Kd6","f":"4k3/R7/3KP3/8/8/8/8/7r b - - 2 3"},{"u":"h1d1","s":"Rd1+","f":"4k3/R7/3KP3/8/8/8/8/3r4 w - - 3 4","n":"<p><span class='mv'>3...Rd1+</span> Checks from behind. The white king can't hide in front of its own pawn, because the pawn is there.</p>"},{"u":"d6e5","s":"Ke5","f":"4k3/R7/4P3/4K3/8/8/8/3r4 b - - 4 4"},{"u":"d1e1","s":"Re1+","f":"4k3/R7/4P3/4K3/8/8/8/4r3 w - - 5 5"},{"u":"e5d6","s":"Kd6","f":"4k3/R7/3KP3/8/8/8/8/4r3 b - - 6 5"},{"u":"e1d1","s":"Rd1+","f":"4k3/R7/3KP3/8/8/8/8/3r4 w - - 7 6","n":"<p><span class='mv'>5...Rd1+</span> White can't escape the checks. Draw.</p>"}],"mate":false,"end":"4k3/R7/3KP3/8/8/8/8/3r4 w - - 7 6"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"5k2/R7/2r5/4KP2/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 1","orient":"b","title":"Hold the fence","task":"You're Black. Hold the draw for 12 moves against Stockfish.","goal":{"type":"hold","n":12},"hints":["Keep your rook on the 6th rank while the pawn is on the 5th.","Keep your king in front of the pawn.","When the pawn steps to f6, put your rook on the 1st rank and check from behind."],"win":"Fence first, checks from behind later. You've just learned the defence that saves more half-points than any other."}},{"id":"lucena","level":"L3","kick":"Lesson 11 · rook endings","title":"Building a bridge","metaphor":"To cross a river under fire, build a bridge before you step out.","story":["Now the other side. Your pawn is on the 7th rank, your king sits on the promotion square in front of it, and the enemy king is cut off. You're one step from a queen, but your own king is in the way, and the moment it steps out, the enemy rook checks it from the side, again and again.","The position is named after Lucena, a Spanish author from 1497, though it doesn't appear in his book; it first shows up in Salvio's book of 1634. The winning idea has a beautiful name: <b>building a bridge</b>."],"principle":"Use your rook to push the defending king one more file away, then put your rook on the <b>4th rank</b> (counting from your side). Now step your king out. The enemy rook checks; your king walks towards the rook; and when the checks run out, your rook steps in between and blocks: the bridge. Then the pawn promotes.","tells":["King in front of your own pawn on the 7th, enemy king cut off by a file.","Rook to the 4th rank before the king steps out.","Walk your king towards your rook, then block the last check."],"twist":{"q":"Why the 4th rank? Why not the 3rd or 5th?","a":"The king walks down two ranks (8th to 6th or 5th) while the enemy rook checks. On the 4th rank, your rook is exactly where it can block the last check along the file with the king protecting it. It's geometry, and on some files the 5th rank works too, but the 4th rank is the reliable rule."},"demos":[{"title":"The bridge","src":"Lucena position · Stockfish-checked","fen":"3K4/3P1k2/8/8/8/8/2r5/4R3 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>White's king is stuck in front of the d-pawn. The black rook on c2 guards the c-file, and the black king guards e7 and e8.</p>","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"e1f1","s":"Rf1+","f":"3K4/3P1k2/8/8/8/8/2r5/5R2 b - - 1 1","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1.Rf1+</span> Push the black king one file further away.</p>"},{"u":"f7g6","s":"Kg6","f":"3K4/3P4/6k1/8/8/8/2r5/5R2 w - - 2 2"},{"u":"f1f4","s":"Rf4","f":"3K4/3P4/6k1/8/5R2/8/2r5/8 b - - 3 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>2.Rf4!</span> The bridge is prepared on the 4th rank. Nothing happens yet. Wait for it.</p>","a":[{"u":"f4d4","k":"c"}]},{"u":"c2c1","s":"Rc1","f":"3K4/3P4/6k1/8/5R2/8/8/2r5 w - - 4 3"},{"u":"d8e7","s":"Ke7","f":"8/3PK3/6k1/8/5R2/8/8/2r5 b - - 5 3","n":"<p><span class='mv'>3.Ke7</span> Now the king steps out. The checks begin.</p>"},{"u":"c1e1","s":"Re1+","f":"8/3PK3/6k1/8/5R2/8/8/4r3 w - - 6 4"},{"u":"e7d6","s":"Kd6","f":"8/3P4/3K2k1/8/5R2/8/8/4r3 b - - 7 4","n":"<p><span class='mv'>4.Kd6</span> The king walks down towards its rook.</p>"},{"u":"e1d1","s":"Rd1+","f":"8/3P4/3K2k1/8/5R2/8/8/3r4 w - - 8 5"},{"u":"d6e6","s":"Ke6","f":"8/3P4/4K1k1/8/5R2/8/8/3r4 b - - 9 5"},{"u":"d1e1","s":"Re1+","f":"8/3P4/4K1k1/8/5R2/8/8/4r3 w - - 10 6"},{"u":"e6d5","s":"Kd5","f":"8/3P4/6k1/3K4/5R2/8/8/4r3 b - - 11 6","n":"<p><span class='mv'>6.Kd5</span> One more check is coming, on the d-file.</p>"},{"u":"e1d1","s":"Rd1+","f":"8/3P4/6k1/3K4/5R2/8/8/3r4 w - - 12 7"},{"u":"f4d4","s":"Rd4","f":"8/3P4/6k1/3K4/3R4/8/8/3r4 b - - 13 7","n":"<p><span class='mv'>7.Rd4!</span> The bridge. The checks are over, the rook is protected by the king, and d8=Q is next. Black can't stop it.</p>"}],"mate":false,"end":"8/3P4/6k1/3K4/3R4/8/8/3r4 b - - 13 7"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"1K6/1P1k4/8/8/8/8/r7/2R5 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","title":"Build your own bridge","task":"Win: promote the b-pawn and keep the queen. Stockfish defends.","goal":{"type":"promote"},"hints":["First push the black king another file away with a rook check.","Then rook to your 4th rank.","Step out with your king, walk it towards the rook, block the last check with the rook."],"win":"Check, bridge, walk, block. That's the Lucena."}},{"id":"tarrasch","level":"L3","kick":"Lesson 12 · rook endings","title":"Behind the passed pawn","metaphor":"Push the cart from behind. Don't stand in front of it.","story":["You can move a cart by pulling it from the front or pushing it from behind. Push it from behind and you see the whole road. Pull it from the front, and every step forward the cart takes is one step backward for you.","Siegbert Tarrasch turned this into a rule: <b>rooks belong behind passed pawns</b>. Yours, to push it. Your opponent's, to stop it. A rook behind a pawn gets more active as the pawn advances. A rook in front of it gets more passive."],"principle":"A rook behind its own passed pawn supports every step, and as the pawn advances, the rook's file gets longer. The enemy rook in front of the pawn has to stay there as a blocker, and that rook is passive: it can't attack anything. Meanwhile your king is free to walk wherever it wants.","tells":["You have a passed pawn and the enemy rook is in front of it.","Can your rook reach the file behind your pawn?","His rook is tied down; now your king is free."],"twist":{"q":"Your opponent has the passed pawn. Where should YOUR rook go?","a":"Also behind it. From behind, your rook attacks the pawn and gets <i>more</i> active as the pawn advances (it controls more squares on the file), while the enemy rook has to defend the pawn passively. The rule works for both sides."},"demos":[{"title":"The active rook and the prisoner","src":"Technique demo · Stockfish-checked","fen":"r5k1/5pp1/7p/P7/8/6P1/5PK1/R7 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>White's rook is behind the a-pawn, Black's rook is in front of it. Watch which rook ends up doing something.</p>","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"a5a6","s":"a6","f":"r5k1/5pp1/P6p/8/8/6P1/5PK1/R7 b - - 0 1","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1.a6</span> Every step makes White's rook stronger: the a-file behind the pawn gets longer.</p>"},{"u":"g8f8","s":"Kf8","f":"r4k2/5pp1/P6p/8/8/6P1/5PK1/R7 w - - 1 2"},{"u":"f2f4","s":"f4","f":"r4k2/5pp1/P6p/8/5P2/6P1/6K1/R7 b - - 0 2"},{"u":"f8e7","s":"Ke7","f":"r7/4kpp1/P6p/8/5P2/6P1/6K1/R7 w - - 1 3"},{"u":"g2f3","s":"Kf3","f":"r7/4kpp1/P6p/8/5P2/5KP1/8/R7 b - - 2 3"},{"u":"a8a7","s":"Ra7","f":"8/r3kpp1/P6p/8/5P2/5KP1/8/R7 w - - 3 4","n":"<p><span class='mv'>3...Ra7</span> The black rook is a prisoner. It can't leave the a-file or the pawn runs.</p>"},{"u":"f3e4","s":"Ke4","f":"8/r3kpp1/P6p/8/4KP2/6P1/8/R7 b - - 4 4","n":"<p><span class='mv'>4.Ke4</span> White's king is completely free to walk.</p>"},{"u":"e7d6","s":"Kd6","f":"8/r4pp1/P2k3p/8/4KP2/6P1/8/R7 w - - 5 5"},{"u":"f4f5","s":"f5","f":"8/r4pp1/P2k3p/5P2/4K3/6P1/8/R7 b - - 0 5"},{"u":"d6c6","s":"Kc6","f":"8/r4pp1/P1k4p/5P2/4K3/6P1/8/R7 w - - 1 6"},{"u":"e4f4","s":"Kf4","f":"8/r4pp1/P1k4p/5P2/5K2/6P1/8/R7 b - - 2 6"},{"u":"c6d6","s":"Kd6","f":"8/r4pp1/P2k3p/5P2/5K2/6P1/8/R7 w - - 3 7"},{"u":"a1a3","s":"Ra3","f":"8/r4pp1/P2k3p/5P2/5K2/R5P1/8/8 b - - 4 7"},{"u":"d6c6","s":"Kc6","f":"8/r4pp1/P1k4p/5P2/5K2/R5P1/8/8 w - - 5 8","n":"<p><span class='mv'>7...Kc6</span> Black's king has had to go to the queenside to help. The kingside is left to White's king. White is clearly better, and Black can only wait.</p>"}],"mate":false,"end":"8/r4pp1/P1k4p/5P2/5K2/R5P1/8/8 w - - 5 8"}],"challenge":{"type":"choice","fen":"8/r4pk1/6p1/8/P7/6P1/5PK1/1R6 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","title":"Where does the rook go?","task":"White to move. Black's rook is in front of your a-pawn. Choose.","options":[{"san":"Ra1","ok":true,"why":"Behind the passed pawn. The pawn is protected forever, and every step it takes makes the rook stronger. Black's rook stays tied to the a-file.","u":"b1a1","f":"8/r4pk1/6p1/8/P7/6P1/5PK1/R7 b - - 1 1"},{"san":"a5","ok":false,"why":"1...Rxa5. The pawn wasn't protected. Pawn gone, advantage gone.","u":"a4a5","f":"8/r4pk1/6p1/P7/8/6P1/5PK1/1R6 b - - 0 1"},{"san":"Kf3","ok":false,"why":"1...Rxa4. Your king walked towards the centre, but your pawn was hanging. Equal.","u":"g2f3","f":"8/r4pk1/6p1/8/P7/5KP1/5P2/1R6 b - - 1 1"}],"win":"Rooks belong behind passed pawns. White keeps a lasting advantage: the black rook can only watch.","steps":[{"u":"b1a1","s":"Ra1","f":"8/r4pk1/6p1/8/P7/6P1/5PK1/R7 b - - 1 1"},{"u":"a7a5","s":"Ra5","f":"8/5pk1/6p1/r7/P7/6P1/5PK1/R7 w - - 2 2"},{"u":"g2f3","s":"Kf3","f":"8/5pk1/6p1/r7/P7/5KP1/5P2/R7 b - - 3 2"},{"u":"g7f6","s":"Kf6","f":"8/5p2/5kp1/r7/P7/5KP1/5P2/R7 w - - 4 3"},{"u":"f3e4","s":"Ke4","f":"8/5p2/5kp1/r7/P3K3/6P1/5P2/R7 b - - 5 3"},{"u":"f6e6","s":"Ke6","f":"8/5p2/4k1p1/r7/P3K3/6P1/5P2/R7 w - - 6 4"}]}},{"id":"wrongcorner","level":"L3","kick":"Lesson 13 · minor pieces","title":"The wrong corner","metaphor":"A key that fits every lock except the one door you need.","story":["A bishop and an extra pawn should win. A whole piece and a pawn, against a bare king! But there's one famous exception.","If the pawn is a <b>rook pawn</b> (a- or h-pawn) and the bishop doesn't control the colour of the promotion square, the defending king simply sits in the corner. The bishop can never chase it out, and when the attacker tries too hard, the result is stalemate. The bishop is a key that fits every lock except that one door."],"principle":"Look at the colour of the queening square. h8 and a1 are dark squares; a8 and h1 are light. A bishop that can't control the queening square of a rook pawn can never drive the defending king out of that corner. So the defender just runs to the corner, and the game is a draw, no matter how many moves are left. As the defender: race for the corner. As the attacker: keep his king out of it, or trade into a different ending.","tells":["A rook pawn plus a bishop that doesn't control the promotion square.","Defender: the corner is your fortress. Get there.","Attacker: watch out for stalemate tricks."],"twist":{"q":"Same position, but the white bishop is dark-squared. What changes?","a":"Everything. A dark bishop controls h8, so it can drive the king out of the corner, or simply cover h8 so the pawn promotes. Then the ending is an easy win. The colour of one square decides the result."},"demos":[{"title":"Into the fortress","src":"Technique demo · Stockfish-checked","fen":"8/3k4/8/8/2B4P/5K2/8/8 b - - 0 1","orient":"b","n0":"<p>White has a whole extra bishop and an h-pawn. But h8 is a dark square, and the bishop on c4 lives on light squares. Black's only job: reach h8.</p>","a0":[],"m0":["h8"],"steps":[{"u":"d7e7","s":"Ke7","f":"8/4k3/8/8/2B4P/5K2/8/8 w - - 1 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1...Ke7</span> Run to the corner, and run diagonally.</p>"},{"u":"h4h5","s":"h5","f":"8/4k3/8/7P/2B5/5K2/8/8 b - - 0 2"},{"u":"e7f6","s":"Kf6","f":"8/8/5k2/7P/2B5/5K2/8/8 w - - 1 3"},{"u":"f3f4","s":"Kf4","f":"8/8/5k2/7P/2B2K2/8/8/8 b - - 2 3"},{"u":"f6g7","s":"Kg7","f":"8/6k1/8/7P/2B2K2/8/8/8 w - - 3 4","n":"<p><span class='mv'>3...Kg7</span> Almost there. The white king came too late to keep it out.</p>"},{"u":"f4g5","s":"Kg5","f":"8/6k1/8/6KP/2B5/8/8/8 b - - 4 4"},{"u":"g7h8","s":"Kh8","f":"7k/8/8/6KP/2B5/8/8/8 w - - 5 5","n":"<p><span class='mv'>4...Kh8</span> Home. From now on Black shuffles between h8, g8 and h7.</p>"},{"u":"h5h6","s":"h6","f":"7k/8/7P/6K1/2B5/8/8/8 b - - 0 5"},{"u":"h8h7","s":"Kh7","f":"8/7k/7P/6K1/2B5/8/8/8 w - - 1 6","n":"<p><span class='mv'>5...Kh7</span> The light bishop can take g8 and h7 away, but never h8. If White takes away everything, it's stalemate. Draw, a full piece down.</p>"}],"mate":false,"end":"8/7k/7P/6K1/2B5/8/8/8 w - - 1 6"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"8/8/8/3k4/7P/3B1K2/8/8 b - - 0 1","orient":"b","title":"Race to the fortress","task":"You're Black. Reach the h8 corner (or capture the pawn). Only one first move works.","goal":{"type":"reach","sq":["h8"],"n":15},"hints":["White's king on f3 will try to block your path.","Which square brings you closer to h8 and keeps the white king from shouldering you aside?","1...Ke6! Then f7, g7, h8."],"win":"1...Ke6! and the fortress is yours. A whole piece up, and White can't win."}},{"id":"ocb","level":"L3","kick":"Lesson 14 · minor pieces","title":"Two bishops that never meet","metaphor":"Two guards on different floors of the same building never meet.","story":["Two night guards patrol the same building, one on the even floors, one on the odd floors. They can walk past each other forever and never meet.","Bishops of <b>opposite colours</b> are those guards. One controls light squares, the other dark squares, so they can never attack or block each other. That makes these endings famously drawish: a defender can be one, sometimes even two, pawns down and still hold, by blockading the pawns on the squares his bishop controls."],"principle":"With opposite-coloured bishops, blockade the enemy pawns on <b>your</b> bishop's colour. Your bishop then controls the squares the pawns need, and his bishop can never challenge it. Put your king in front of the pawns on the other colour. Two pawns that can't advance are worth nothing.","tells":["Opposite-coloured bishops: defender, think fortress.","Your king blockades on one colour, your bishop on the other.","Don't hunt pawns. Just stop them moving."],"twist":{"q":"Why are opposite-coloured bishops dangerous for the defender in the middlegame, when they're so drawish in the endgame?","a":"In the middlegame, the attacker's bishop attacks squares that the defender's bishop can never defend. So an attack on the king with opposite bishops is like having an extra piece. In the endgame there's no king to attack, so the same property becomes a fortress."},"demos":[{"title":"Two pawns up, no progress","src":"Technique demo · Stockfish-checked","fen":"8/3bk3/8/4PP2/5K2/2B5/8/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>White is two pawns up. But the black king blocks e6 and f6 and the light-squared bishop guards e6 and f7. White's dark bishop can never attack those light squares.</p>","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"f4g5","s":"Kg5","f":"8/3bk3/8/4PPK1/8/2B5/8/8 b - - 1 1","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1.Kg5</span> White's king tries to help.</p>"},{"u":"e7f7","s":"Kf7","f":"8/3b1k2/8/4PPK1/8/2B5/8/8 w - - 2 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1...Kf7</span> Black just holds the line.</p>"},{"u":"c3b4","s":"Bb4","f":"8/3b1k2/8/4PPK1/1B6/8/8/8 b - - 3 2"},{"u":"d7c8","s":"Bc8","f":"2b5/5k2/8/4PPK1/1B6/8/8/8 w - - 4 3","n":"<p><span class='mv'>2...Bc8</span> The bishop shuffles along the c8–h3 diagonal, always guarding e6 and f5.</p>"},{"u":"b4a3","s":"Ba3","f":"2b5/5k2/8/4PPK1/8/B7/8/8 b - - 5 3"},{"u":"c8d7","s":"Bd7","f":"8/3b1k2/8/4PPK1/8/B7/8/8 w - - 6 4"},{"u":"a3c5","s":"Bc5","f":"8/3b1k2/8/2B1PPK1/8/8/8/8 b - - 7 4"},{"u":"d7c8","s":"Bc8","f":"2b5/5k2/8/2B1PPK1/8/8/8/8 w - - 8 5"},{"u":"c5g1","s":"Bg1","f":"2b5/5k2/8/4PPK1/8/8/8/6B1 b - - 9 5"},{"u":"c8d7","s":"Bd7","f":"8/3b1k2/8/4PPK1/8/8/8/6B1 w - - 10 6"},{"u":"g1c5","s":"Bc5","f":"8/3b1k2/8/2B1PPK1/8/8/8/8 b - - 11 6"},{"u":"d7c8","s":"Bc8","f":"2b5/5k2/8/2B1PPK1/8/8/8/8 w - - 12 7"},{"u":"c5b4","s":"Bb4","f":"2b5/5k2/8/4PPK1/1B6/8/8/8 b - - 13 7"},{"u":"c8d7","s":"Bd7","f":"8/3b1k2/8/4PPK1/1B6/8/8/8 w - - 14 8"},{"u":"g5g4","s":"Kg4","f":"8/3b1k2/8/4PP2/1B4K1/8/8/8 b - - 15 8"},{"u":"d7c8","s":"Bc8","f":"2b5/5k2/8/4PP2/1B4K1/8/8/8 w - - 16 9","n":"<p><span class='mv'>8...Bc8</span> White has tried everything. If e6 or f6 are pushed, the pawns get blocked or traded. Two pawns up, and it's a draw.</p>"}],"mate":false,"end":"2b5/5k2/8/4PP2/1B4K1/8/8/8 w - - 16 9"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"8/3kb3/8/2PP4/2K5/5B2/8/8 b - - 0 1","orient":"b","title":"Build the fortress","task":"You're Black, two pawns down. Hold the draw for 12 moves.","goal":{"type":"hold","n":12},"hints":["White's pawns want to reach c6 and d6. Which of those does your bishop control?","Keep your king on c7/d7, and your bishop on the f8–a3 and d8–h4 diagonals.","Don't wander. A bishop move like Bf8 or Be7 that keeps watching d6 is all you need."],"win":"You can't win the pawns, but you don't need to. A blockade is a draw."}},{"id":"reti","level":"L4","kick":"Lesson 15 · composition","title":"Chasing two hares","metaphor":"The hunter who chases two hares catches neither. Unless he runs on the diagonal.","story":["In 1921 Richard Réti published a position that looks like a joke. White's king is in the corner, hopelessly far from the black pawn, which runs freely to promote. White's own pawn is about to be eaten. Any beginner would resign.","But the king walks the diagonal, and every step does <b>two jobs</b>: it chases the black pawn and supports the white one at the same time. It's the rule of the square from Lesson 3, turned into poetry."],"principle":"A king can move towards two different targets at once, because diagonal steps cost no more than straight ones. If one king move threatens two things, the opponent can only deal with one. In king-and-pawn races, before you give up, look for a <b>diagonal route that serves two goals</b>.","tells":["Your king seems too far from everything.","There's a diagonal path between your two targets.","Make each move threaten to do something useful: catch his pawn or support yours."],"twist":{"q":"In the Réti study, why does 1.c7? lose immediately?","a":"1...Kb7 and the c-pawn falls with no compensation. Then the h-pawn just runs. White's c-pawn is useful only as a <i>threat</i> that forces Black to spend time. Pushing it gives up that threat."},"demos":[{"title":"Réti, 1921","src":"Richard Réti · 1921 · Stockfish-checked","fen":"7K/8/k1P5/7p/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>White to move and draw. The h-pawn is miles from the white king, and the c-pawn looks lost. Impossible?</p>","a0":[{"u":"h8e5","k":"c"}],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"h8g7","s":"Kg7","f":"8/6K1/k1P5/7p/8/8/8/8 b - - 1 1","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1.Kg7</span> Toward the h-pawn, along the diagonal.</p>"},{"u":"h5h4","s":"h4","f":"8/6K1/k1P5/8/7p/8/8/8 w - - 0 2"},{"u":"g7f6","s":"Kf6","f":"8/8/k1P2K2/8/7p/8/8/8 b - - 1 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>2.Kf6</span> Still diagonal: still chasing h4, and now also heading for the c-pawn. If 2...h3 3.Ke7! h2 4.c7 and both pawns queen: draw.</p>"},{"u":"a6b6","s":"Kb6","f":"8/8/1kP2K2/8/7p/8/8/8 w - - 2 3","n":"<p><span class='mv'>2...Kb6</span> Black stops the c-pawn first.</p>"},{"u":"f6e5","s":"Ke5","f":"8/8/1kP5/4K3/7p/8/8/8 b - - 3 3","n":"<p><span class='mv'>3.Ke5!</span> The punchline. The king is now inside the square of the h-pawn <i>and</i> close enough to defend c6.</p>"},{"u":"b6c6","s":"Kxc6","f":"8/8/2k5/4K3/7p/8/8/8 w - - 0 4"},{"u":"e5f4","s":"Kf4","f":"8/8/2k5/8/5K1p/8/8/8 b - - 1 4","n":"<p><span class='mv'>4.Kf4</span> The king catches the h-pawn. Draw. A king that was hopelessly late for two races arrived in time for both.</p>"}],"mate":false,"end":"8/8/2k5/8/5K1p/8/8/8 b - - 1 4"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"K7/8/5P1k/p7/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","title":"Two hares, mirrored","task":"Draw. Only one first move saves White.","goal":{"type":"hold","n":10},"hints":["Pushing f7 loses the pawn at once, and running straight after the a-pawn is too slow.","Find the diagonal that heads towards the a-pawn and towards your f-pawn at the same time.","1.Kb7! Then keep threatening both."],"win":"1.Kb7! Réti's idea in a new position. You chased two hares and caught them both."}},{"id":"mined","level":"L4","kick":"Lesson 16 · composition","title":"The mined squares","metaphor":"A minefield where the first one to step forward loses.","story":["The <b>trébuchet</b> is named after the medieval siege engine. Two kings each attack the other's pawn and each defend their own. Whoever has to move must abandon his pawn and lose it. It's a minefield where both players stand still and the first to step forward blows up.","In more complex endings, the same idea becomes <b>corresponding squares</b>: for every square your king stands on, there's a matching square where his king must stand. The player who gets there with the opponent to move wins. Sometimes that means taking a strange detour to arrive one tempo later, or earlier."],"principle":"In a mutual zugzwang, whoever has to move loses. So the fight is not about the zugzwang itself, but about <b>who arrives in it with the turn</b>. Work backwards: find the mined position, then find the route that gets your king there with your opponent to move. The obvious route often arrives with the wrong tempo.","tells":["Both kings are tied to defending something.","Count tempi: who will have to move when the kings arrive?","If the direct route arrives with the wrong tempo, find a route that takes one move longer."],"twist":{"q":"Why is triangulation (walking a king around three squares to come back to the same square) related to this?","a":"Triangulation is a way to lose a tempo on purpose: your king takes three moves to return to its square, while his king, with fewer free squares, needs only two, or can't follow at all. You arrive at the same position with the opponent to move. It's the same idea as the detour: change who has the move."},"demos":[{"title":"The trébuchet, White to move","src":"Mutual zugzwang · Stockfish-checked","fen":"8/8/2Kp4/3Pk3/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>White's king attacks d6 and defends d5. Black's king attacks d5 and defends d6. It's White's move.</p>","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"c6b6","s":"Kb6","f":"8/8/1K1p4/3Pk3/8/8/8/8 b - - 1 1","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1.Kb6</span> White has to move and every king move abandons d5.</p>"},{"u":"e5d5","s":"Kxd5","f":"8/8/1K1p4/3k4/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 2","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1...Kxd5</span> Black wins the pawn, and now Black's king stands in front of its own pawn. That's a winning king-and-pawn ending (Lesson 4).</p>"},{"u":"b6b5","s":"Kb5","f":"8/8/3p4/1K1k4/8/8/8/8 b - - 1 2"},{"u":"d5e4","s":"Ke4","f":"8/8/3p4/1K6/4k3/8/8/8 w - - 2 3","n":"<p><span class='mv'>2...Ke4</span> Black will promote. Now flip it: with <b>Black</b> to move in the starting position, Black loses the d6-pawn instead. Same squares, opposite result.</p>"}],"mate":false,"end":"8/8/3p4/1K6/4k3/8/8/8 w - - 2 3"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"8/3k4/3p4/3P4/8/2K5/8/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","title":"Arrive with the right tempo","task":"Win: promote and keep the queen. The natural moves 1.Kc4 and 1.Kd4 only draw.","goal":{"type":"promote"},"hints":["You want to attack d6 from c6 or e6 at a moment when Black's king can't defend it.","The direct route arrives one tempo wrong. The black king just mirrors you.","1.Kb4! A detour via a5/b5 changes the count."],"win":"1.Kb4! A detour that looks like a waste of time is exactly the tempo that wins."}},{"id":"saavedra","level":"L4","kick":"Lesson 17 · composition","title":"The rook that saved the game","metaphor":"Sometimes asking for less gets you more.","story":["In 1895 a Glasgow newspaper printed an endgame by Georges Barbier. White's pawn promotes, and the black rook sets a stalemate trap. Barbier concluded: draw.","A Spanish priest living in Glasgow, Fernando Saavedra, looked again and found that White should <b>not</b> make a queen. A rook avoids the stalemate and sets a mating threat of its own. It's probably the most famous endgame study ever composed."],"principle":"Promotion doesn't have to be a queen. A queen controls so much that she can accidentally <b>stalemate</b> the enemy king, or walk into a stalemate trick. When the opponent's only defence is stalemate, check whether a smaller piece (a rook, sometimes a knight) wins instead.","tells":["You're about to promote and his king has almost no moves.","His defence relies on sacrificing his last piece for stalemate.","Ask: what does the queen do here that a rook wouldn't?"],"twist":{"q":"After 6.c8=R Ra4, why does 7.Kb3 win?","a":"It attacks the rook on a4 and threatens Rc1 mate at the same time. Black can't do both: if the rook moves, Rc1 is mate; if Black prevents mate, the rook is lost."},"demos":[{"title":"Barbier's draw: the obvious queen","src":"Barbier · Glasgow Weekly Citizen, 1895 · Stockfish-checked","fen":"8/8/1KP5/3r4/8/8/8/k7 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>White's c-pawn is about to promote. The black rook checks the white king down the board. Watch what happens if White makes a queen.</p>","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"c6c7","s":"c7","f":"8/2P5/1K6/3r4/8/8/8/k7 b - - 0 1","n":"<p><span class='mv'>1.c7</span> Black can't stop the pawn, so he checks.</p>"},{"u":"d5d6","s":"Rd6+","f":"8/2P5/1K1r4/8/8/8/8/k7 w - - 1 2"},{"u":"b6b5","s":"Kb5","f":"8/2P5/3r4/1K6/8/8/8/k7 b - - 2 2"},{"u":"d6d5","s":"Rd5+","f":"8/2P5/8/1K1r4/8/8/8/k7 w - - 3 3"},{"u":"b5b4","s":"Kb4","f":"8/2P5/8/3r4/1K6/8/8/k7 b - - 4 3"},{"u":"d5d4","s":"Rd4+","f":"8/2P5/8/8/1K1r4/8/8/k7 w - - 5 4"},{"u":"b4b3","s":"Kb3","f":"8/2P5/8/8/3r4/1K6/8/k7 b - - 6 4"},{"u":"d4d3","s":"Rd3+","f":"8/2P5/8/8/8/1K1r4/8/k7 w - - 7 5"},{"u":"b3c2","s":"Kc2","f":"8/2P5/8/8/8/3r4/2K5/k7 b - - 8 5","n":"<p><span class='mv'>5.Kc2</span> The king finally escapes the checks: 5...Rd2+ would just lose the rook to Kxd2...</p>"},{"u":"d3d4","s":"Rd4","f":"8/2P5/8/8/3r4/8/2K5/k7 w - - 9 6","n":"<p><span class='mv'>5...Rd4!</span> ...so it sets a trap: if 6.c8=Q, then 6...Rc4+!</p>"},{"u":"c7c8q","s":"c8=Q","f":"2Q5/8/8/8/3r4/8/2K5/k7 b - - 0 6","n":"<p><span class='mv'>6.c8=Q?</span> The natural move.</p>"},{"u":"d4c4","s":"Rc4+","f":"2Q5/8/8/8/2r5/8/2K5/k7 w - - 1 7"},{"u":"c8c4","s":"Qxc4","f":"8/8/8/8/2Q5/8/2K5/k7 b - - 0 7","n":"<p><span class='mv'>7.Qxc4</span> <b>Stalemate.</b> The black king on a1 has no legal moves. Barbier stopped here and called it a draw. Your challenge: find what Saavedra found on move 6.</p>"}],"mate":false,"end":"8/8/8/8/2Q5/8/2K5/k7 b - - 0 7"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"8/8/1KP5/3r4/8/8/8/k7 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","title":"Saavedra, 1895","task":"White to move and win. Win the black rook (or mate). Stockfish defends.","goal":{"type":"material"},"hints":["Start as in the demo: push and walk your king down to c2 to escape the checks.","On move 6, a queen gets stalemated. What other piece stops ...Rc4+ from working?","6.c8=R!! threatens Ra8 mate. Black's rook must defend, and then 7.Kb3 wins it."],"win":"6.c8=R!! The underpromotion that made a priest immortal."}},{"id":"kbn","level":"L4","kick":"Lesson 18 · the final exam","title":"The bishop, the knight and the W","metaphor":"Two dancers who can't see each other's steps, moving as one.","story":["King, bishop and knight against a king is the hardest basic mate. It can take 33 moves, and the 50-move rule is waiting. Many grandmasters have failed to finish it in tournament games.","The secret: you can only force mate in a corner of the <b>same colour as your bishop</b>. If the king runs to the wrong corner, you must drive it along the edge to the right one. The knight traces a W shape while the bishop and king hold the walls. Treat this lesson as your final exam."],"principle":"First push the king to the edge. Then, if it's in the wrong corner (opposite colour to your bishop), drive it along the edge towards the right corner: the bishop takes squares of its colour, the knight covers the other colour, and your king stays close, a knight's move in front. In the right corner, the bishop and knight take turns covering the last squares until mate.","tells":["Which corner matches your bishop's colour? That's where mate happens.","Bishop and knight cover different colours: they complement each other.","Keep your king close to his. All three pieces must work together."],"twist":{"q":"Why can't you mate in the wrong corner?","a":"In the corner, the king needs its corner square and the two next to it covered, plus the check. A bishop of the wrong colour can't give check on the corner square's diagonal and can't cover it. You'd need the knight to give mate while the bishop covers everything else, and that can't be forced against good defence."},"demos":[{"title":"From the wrong corner to the right one","src":"Technique demo · Stockfish-checked","fen":"8/8/k1KB4/8/8/8/2N5/8 b - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"<p>White's bishop is dark-squared, so mate is only possible in a dark corner: a1 or h8. The black king is near a8, a <b>light</b> corner. It must be marched down the a-file to a1.</p>","a0":[],"m0":["a1","a8"],"steps":[{"u":"a6a7","s":"Ka7","f":"8/k7/2KB4/8/8/8/2N5/8 w - - 1 2"},{"u":"c2a3","s":"Na3","f":"8/k7/2KB4/8/8/N7/8/8 b - - 2 2"},{"u":"a7a8","s":"Ka8","f":"k7/8/2KB4/8/8/N7/8/8 w - - 3 3"},{"u":"a3c4","s":"Nc4","f":"k7/8/2KB4/8/2N5/8/8/8 b - - 4 3","n":"<p><span class='mv'>3.Nc4</span> The knight heads for b6 to take squares from the black king.</p>"},{"u":"a8a7","s":"Ka7","f":"8/k7/2KB4/8/2N5/8/8/8 w - - 5 4"},{"u":"c4b6","s":"Nb6","f":"8/k7/1NKB4/8/8/8/8/8 b - - 6 4"},{"u":"a7a6","s":"Ka6","f":"8/8/kNKB4/8/8/8/8/8 w - - 7 5"},{"u":"d6b8","s":"Bb8","f":"1B6/8/kNK5/8/8/8/8/8 b - - 8 5","n":"<p><span class='mv'>5.Bb8</span> The bishop takes a7 and the king is forced down the a-file.</p>"},{"u":"a6a5","s":"Ka5","f":"1B6/8/1NK5/k7/8/8/8/8 w - - 9 6"},{"u":"b6d5","s":"Nd5","f":"1B6/8/2K5/k2N4/8/8/8/8 b - - 10 6"},{"u":"a5a4","s":"Ka4","f":"1B6/8/2K5/3N4/k7/8/8/8 w - - 11 7"},{"u":"c6c5","s":"Kc5","f":"1B6/8/8/2KN4/k7/8/8/8 b - - 12 7","n":"<p><span class='mv'>7.Kc5</span> The white king steps up to stay close and block the road to the centre.</p>"},{"u":"a4b3","s":"Kb3","f":"1B6/8/8/2KN4/8/1k6/8/8 w - - 13 8","n":"<p><span class='mv'>7...Kb3</span> The king tries to escape towards the centre. The pieces regroup to build a new net.</p>"},{"u":"d5b4","s":"Nb4","f":"1B6/8/8/2K5/1N6/1k6/8/8 b - - 14 8"},{"u":"b3b2","s":"Kb2","f":"1B6/8/8/2K5/1N6/8/1k6/8 w - - 15 9"},{"u":"b8f4","s":"Bf4","f":"8/8/8/2K5/1N3B2/8/1k6/8 b - - 16 9"},{"u":"b2c3","s":"Kc3","f":"8/8/8/2K5/1N3B2/2k5/8/8 w - - 17 10"},{"u":"f4g5","s":"Bg5","f":"8/8/8/2K3B1/1N6/2k5/8/8 b - - 18 10"},{"u":"c3b3","s":"Kb3","f":"8/8/8/2K3B1/1N6/1k6/8/8 w - - 19 11"},{"u":"g5f6","s":"Bf6","f":"8/8/5B2/2K5/1N6/1k6/8/8 b - - 20 11"},{"u":"b3a4","s":"Ka4","f":"8/8/5B2/2K5/kN6/8/8/8 w - - 21 12"},{"u":"c5c4","s":"Kc4","f":"8/8/5B2/8/kNK5/8/8/8 b - - 22 12"},{"u":"a4a5","s":"Ka5","f":"8/8/5B2/k7/1NK5/8/8/8 w - - 23 13"},{"u":"f6d8","s":"Bd8+","f":"3B4/8/8/k7/1NK5/8/8/8 b - - 24 13","n":"<p><span class='mv'>13.Bd8+</span> The bishop pushes the king back to the a-file.</p>"},{"u":"a5a4","s":"Ka4","f":"3B4/8/8/8/kNK5/8/8/8 w - - 25 14"},{"u":"b4d3","s":"Nd3","f":"3B4/8/8/8/k1K5/3N4/8/8 b - - 26 14"},{"u":"a4a3","s":"Ka3","f":"3B4/8/8/8/2K5/k2N4/8/8 w - - 27 15"},{"u":"d8g5","s":"Bg5","f":"8/8/8/6B1/2K5/k2N4/8/8 b - - 28 15"},{"u":"a3a4","s":"Ka4","f":"8/8/8/6B1/k1K5/3N4/8/8 w - - 29 16"},{"u":"g5d2","s":"Bd2","f":"8/8/8/8/k1K5/3N4/3B4/8 b - - 30 16"},{"u":"a4a3","s":"Ka3","f":"8/8/8/8/2K5/k2N4/3B4/8 w - - 31 17"},{"u":"d2b4","s":"Bb4+","f":"8/8/8/8/1BK5/k2N4/8/8 b - - 32 17","n":"<p><span class='mv'>17.Bb4+</span> The king is driven towards a1, the dark corner.</p>"},{"u":"a3a2","s":"Ka2","f":"8/8/8/8/1BK5/3N4/k7/8 w - - 33 18"},{"u":"c4c3","s":"Kc3","f":"8/8/8/8/1B6/2KN4/k7/8 b - - 34 18"},{"u":"a2b1","s":"Kb1","f":"8/8/8/8/1B6/2KN4/8/1k6 w - - 35 19"},{"u":"c3b3","s":"Kb3","f":"8/8/8/8/1B6/1K1N4/8/1k6 b - - 36 19"},{"u":"b1a1","s":"Ka1","f":"8/8/8/8/1B6/1K1N4/8/k7 w - - 37 20"},{"u":"b3c2","s":"Kc2","f":"8/8/8/8/1B6/3N4/2K5/k7 b - - 38 20"},{"u":"a1a2","s":"Ka2","f":"8/8/8/8/1B6/3N4/k1K5/8 w - - 39 21"},{"u":"d3c1","s":"Nc1+","f":"8/8/8/8/1B6/8/k1K5/2N5 b - - 40 21","n":"<p><span class='mv'>21.Nc1+</span> The knight covers the last light square.</p>"},{"u":"a2a1","s":"Ka1","f":"8/8/8/8/1B6/8/2K5/k1N5 w - - 41 22"},{"u":"b4c3","s":"Bc3#","f":"8/8/8/8/8/2B5/2K5/k1N5 b - - 42 22","n":"<p><span class='mv'>22.Bc3#</span> Mate in the dark corner. Bishop on the long diagonal, knight and king covering everything else.</p>"}],"mate":true,"end":"8/8/8/8/8/2B5/2K5/k1N5 b - - 42 22"}],"challenge":{"type":"play","fen":"1k6/8/2K5/8/8/8/8/3NB3 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","title":"Final exam","task":"Checkmate within 50 moves. Your bishop is dark-squared: aim for a1 or h8. Stockfish defends.","goal":{"type":"mate","limit":50},"coach":false,"hints":["The black king is near a8, a light corner. You need to drive it to a1.","Keep your king a knight's move in front of his. Use the bishop to take dark squares on the a-file and the knight for light squares.","Stuck? Tap 'Show me' to see Stockfish's move, then try to understand why."],"win":"You've passed the hardest basic mate in chess. Many club players never manage it."}}],"gauntlet":[{"id":"g1","level":"L1","lesson":"qmate","title":"Queen mate, from far away","fen":"8/8/8/8/2k5/8/8/K6Q w - - 0 1","orient":"w","goal":{"type":"mate","limit":30},"task":"Checkmate."},{"id":"g2","level":"L1","lesson":"rmate","title":"Rook mate, centre king","fen":"8/8/8/8/4k3/8/8/K6R w - - 0 1","orient":"w","goal":{"type":"mate","limit":40},"task":"Checkmate."},{"id":"g3","level":"L1","lesson":"opp","title":"Opposition, one way only","fen":"8/4k3/8/5K2/8/4P3/8/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","goal":{"type":"promote"},"task":"Win. Only one first move works."},{"id":"g4","level":"L1","lesson":"opp","title":"Before you push","fen":"8/8/8/8/5k2/3K4/4P3/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","goal":{"type":"promote"},"task":"Win. Only one first move works."},{"id":"g5","level":"L1","lesson":"wall","title":"Doorway, from the back rank","fen":"8/5k2/8/3KP3/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 1","orient":"b","goal":{"type":"hold","n":12},"task":"You're Black. Draw. Only one move holds."},{"id":"g6","level":"L2","lesson":"outside","title":"Outside pawn, extra kingside","fen":"8/5ppp/8/3k4/P7/3K4/5PPP/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","goal":{"type":"promote"},"task":"Win: promote and keep the queen."},{"id":"g7","level":"L3","lesson":"lucena","title":"Lucena on the e-file","fen":"4K3/4P1k1/8/8/8/8/3r4/5R2 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","goal":{"type":"promote"},"task":"Win: promote and keep the queen."},{"id":"g8","level":"L3","lesson":"wrongcorner","title":"Fortress race, light bishop","fen":"8/8/8/3k4/7P/5K2/8/1B6 b - - 0 1","orient":"b","goal":{"type":"reach","sq":["h8"],"n":15},"task":"You're Black. Reach h8 (or take the pawn)."},{"id":"g9","level":"L4","lesson":"mined","title":"Mined squares, other side","fen":"8/3k4/3p4/3P4/8/4K3/8/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","goal":{"type":"promote"},"task":"Win. The direct route only draws."},{"id":"g10","level":"L4","lesson":"reti","title":"Réti's ghost","fen":"7K/8/1k1P4/7p/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","goal":{"type":"hold","n":10},"task":"Draw. Pushing the d-pawn loses."}],"hero":{"title":"","src":"","fen":"7K/8/k1P5/7p/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1","orient":"w","n0":"","a0":[],"m0":[],"steps":[{"u":"h8g7","s":"Kg7","f":"8/6K1/k1P5/7p/8/8/8/8 b - - 1 1"},{"u":"h5h4","s":"h4","f":"8/6K1/k1P5/8/7p/8/8/8 w - - 0 2"},{"u":"g7f6","s":"Kf6","f":"8/8/k1P2K2/8/7p/8/8/8 b - - 1 2"},{"u":"a6b6","s":"Kb6","f":"8/8/1kP2K2/8/7p/8/8/8 w - - 2 3"},{"u":"f6e5","s":"Ke5","f":"8/8/1kP5/4K3/7p/8/8/8 b - - 3 3"},{"u":"b6c6","s":"Kxc6","f":"8/8/2k5/4K3/7p/8/8/8 w - - 0 4"},{"u":"e5f4","s":"Kf4","f":"8/8/2k5/8/5K1p/8/8/8 b - - 1 4"}],"mate":false,"end":"8/8/2k5/8/5K1p/8/8/8 b - - 1 4"}};
